INSERT INTO public.tac_academy_courses (slug,title,lessons,questions,answer_key) VALUES
('texas-life','Texas Life Insurance License Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here","html":"<h3>Start here</h3><p>Life insurance protects against the financial effect of someone’s death. Follow the people: who owns the contract, whose life is covered and who gets paid?</p><p>This course prepares for the Texas Life Agent exam (InsTX-Life01) and the life portion of General Lines Life, Accident, Health and HMO (InsTX-LAH05). For the combined exam, take both courses.</p><p><a href=\"#week-one\">1-week path</a> · <a href=\"#week-two\">2-week path</a> · <a href=\"#videos\">Watch the videos</a> · <a href=\"#study-map\">Exam study map</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch the videos","html":"<h3>Watch the videos</h3><ol><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_LlJAbpMNP4\">TDI: What to consider if you need life insurance</a>. Identify two financial needs that could disappear when a loan is paid off or a child becomes independent.</li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iw1GQRSZNZw\">TDI: Understanding universal life</a>. Explain how charges and funding affect how long coverage lasts.</li></ol>"},{"id":"week-one","title":"1-week learning path","html":"<h3>1-week learning path</h3><p>Plan about 2–3 hours daily, with more time if the material is new. Watch both videos by day 2.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1\">Life policy basics and term</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-2\">Whole life and flexible permanent policies</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-3\">Variable and joint coverage</a>. Watch the first course video. Explain the terms before looking at the answer.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-4\">Annuities and settlement payouts</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-5\">Riders and exclusions</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-6\">Contract, beneficiaries and ownership</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-7\">Keeping a policy or using cash value</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-8\">Claims, contestability and delivery</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-9\">Underwriting, privacy and contract law</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-10\">Group, business, retirement and tax</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-11\">Texas life rules</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-12\">Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-13\">Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li>Take the graded review. Rework every miss, then use a fresh full-length timed mock. For the combined exam, complete the Health course too.</li></ol>"},{"id":"week-two","title":"2-week learning path","html":"<h3>2-week learning path</h3><p>Plan about 60–90 minutes a day. Start by recalling yesterday’s rule and end with the lesson check. Allow extra time for a fresh timed mock.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1\">Life policy basics and term</a>. Watch the first course video. Explain the terms before looking at the answer.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2\">Whole life and flexible permanent policies</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3\">Variable and joint coverage</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-4\">Annuities and settlement payouts</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-5\">Riders and exclusions</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-6\">Contract, beneficiaries and ownership</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-7\">Keeping a policy or using cash value</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-8\">Claims, contestability and delivery</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-9\">Underwriting, privacy and contract law</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-10\">Group, business, retirement and tax</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-11\">Texas life rules</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-12\">Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-13\">Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li>Take the graded review. Rework every miss, then use a fresh full-length timed mock. For the combined exam, complete the Health course too.</li></ol>"},{"id":"study-map","title":"Exam study map and references","html":"<h3>Exam study map and references</h3><p>Pearson groups life material into products, contract provisions, application and underwriting, and retirement and related concepts. Use lessons 1–4, 5–7, 8–9 and 10 respectively. Study lessons 11–13 for Texas law.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.pearsonvue.com/content/dam/VUE/vue/en/documents/publications/124401.pdf\">Pearson VUE official Texas content outlines</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/agent/life-agent-apply.html\">TDI application steps</a></li><li><a href=\"https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/\">Texas statutes</a></li><li><a href=\"https://tsinational.com/product/life-health-annuity-agent-licensing-tx/\">TSI National study resources</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.xcelsolutions.com/texas/insurance-license/life-and-health\">XCEL study resources</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.examfx.com/hubfs/L%26H_10-Day_Pearson_Cert.pdf\">ExamFX study calendar and simulation approach</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"lesson-1","title":"Life policy basics and term","html":"<h3>Life policy basics and term</h3><p>The owner controls the contract, the insured is the person whose death triggers the claim, and the beneficiary receives proceeds. A premium pays for coverage. Term covers a stated period. Level term keeps the face amount level, decreasing term reduces it, and annually renewable term can increase premiums at renewal. Return-of-premium term refunds specified premiums if contract conditions are met. Renewal extends coverage and conversion changes eligible term to permanent without new evidence of insurability, subject to deadlines.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Renew means keep. Convert means change.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> A client’s health declines and they want permanent coverage without a new medical exam. What feature matters?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Conversion</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-2","title":"Whole life and flexible permanent policies","html":"<h3>Whole life and flexible permanent policies</h3><p>Ordinary whole life typically has level premiums, guaranteed cash values and lifelong coverage if required premiums are paid. Limited-pay compresses payments into fewer years. Single-premium pays once. Interest-sensitive whole life can reflect current interest under its guarantees. Universal life separates charges and cash value and allows flexible payments within contract limits. Insufficient account value can cause lapse. Indexed life credits interest using an index formula, caps and participation rules. A floor applies to crediting and policy charges can still reduce value.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Whole life schedules premiums. UL needs funding reviews.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which policy generally permits flexible premium funding subject to charges and lapse rules?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Universal life</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-3","title":"Variable and joint coverage","html":"<h3>Variable and joint coverage</h3><p>Variable life uses separate-account investment options and the policyholder bears investment risk on cash value. Variable universal life combines that exposure with flexible premium features. Selling variable products also requires applicable securities authority. Joint first-to-die pays on the first insured’s death. Survivorship pays after the second and can serve estate liquidity goals.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Variable varies with investments. Survivorship waits until both die.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which contract is designed to pay after the second insured dies?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Survivorship life</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-4","title":"Annuities and settlement payouts","html":"<h3>Annuities and settlement payouts</h3><p>An annuity can accept one premium or flexible contributions. Immediate annuities generally start income within one year. Deferred contracts accumulate first. Fixed contracts use insurer guarantees, variable accounts take investment risk, and indexed contracts credit with a formula. Life-only payments can end at death. Life with period certain guarantees the specified minimum period. Joint-and-survivor continues under the elected terms after one person dies. Life insurance settlement methods include lump sum, interest only, fixed period, fixed amount and life income.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Accumulate first, annuitize for an income stream.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which payout continues for a survivor under its terms?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Joint-and-survivor</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-5","title":"Riders and exclusions","html":"<h3>Riders and exclusions</h3><p>Waiver of premium and waiver of monthly deductions address different charges during qualifying disability. A payor rider can waive juvenile policy premiums if the payor dies or becomes disabled. Guaranteed insurability permits specified additional purchases without new evidence. Accidental death or AD&amp;D has accident-based triggers. Term and other-insured riders add coverage. LTC and accelerated benefit riders use defined triggers and can reduce the remaining death benefit. Disability income, cost-of-living and return-of-premium features do what their specific terms state. Check war, aviation and hazardous-occupation exclusions.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Waiver pays the bill. Guaranteed insurability buys more.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which rider lets the owner buy more coverage at specified times without new health evidence?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Guaranteed insurability</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-6","title":"Contract, beneficiaries and ownership","html":"<h3>Contract, beneficiaries and ownership</h3><p>The entire contract includes the policy and attached material identified in it. The insuring clause promises coverage. Consideration includes the application and premiums and the insurer’s promise. The owner can assign rights or change a revocable beneficiary. Irrevocable designations can require consent. A contingent beneficiary is next in line. A class designation describes a group, such as children. Common-disaster wording addresses uncertain order of death. For minors, check payment mechanisms such as a trust or legally authorized custodian. An absolute assignment transfers ownership rights. A collateral assignment secures a debt.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Primary first. Contingent next. Collateral secures a debt.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Who generally has power to change a revocable beneficiary?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Policy owner</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-7","title":"Keeping a policy or using cash value","html":"<h3>Keeping a policy or using cash value</h3><p>Premium mode is frequency. A grace period keeps coverage in force briefly after a missed payment under policy terms. An automatic premium loan borrows against available cash value to cover premium. Reinstatement can require arrears, interest and insurability evidence. Loans accrue interest and reduce net proceeds when unpaid. Withdrawals and partial surrenders reduce policy value. Nonforfeiture choices can include cash surrender, reduced paid-up permanent coverage and extended term. Participating policies may pay nonguaranteed dividends that can be taken in cash, reduce premium, accumulate at interest or buy additions or other options.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Reduced paid-up gets less face amount. Extended term gets less time.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which option buys a smaller permanent policy with no further premiums?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Reduced paid-up</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-8","title":"Claims, contestability and delivery","html":"<h3>Claims, contestability and delivery</h3><p>Free look allows return in the stated period. Incontestability limits specified defenses after the statutory and contractual period, subject to exceptions. Suicide clauses limit early policy claims under their terms. A misstatement of age or sex typically adjusts proceeds to what premium would have purchased. Application signatures and authenticated corrections matter. A conditional receipt can start only the protection its terms permit. Deliver the policy, explain ratings, riders and exclusions, collect any required premium and confirm any required statement of continued insurability.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Receipt means read the conditions.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which provision generally adjusts benefits when age was recorded incorrectly?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Misstatement of age</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-9","title":"Underwriting, privacy and contract law","html":"<h3>Underwriting, privacy and contract law</h3><p>Insurable interest must exist at inception. Investor-originated arrangements commonly called STOLI can lack legitimate interest. Underwriters consider application, exam, MIB and permissible consumer reports, then classify risk. A representation is an applicant statement and a warranty is a contractual assurance. FCRA governs consumer reports and adverse-action notices. Follow applicable HIPAA authorization, HIV consent, GLBA privacy and anti-money-laundering procedures. Contracts need agreement, consideration, competent parties and legal purpose. Insurance is conditional, unilateral in its enforceable promise, generally drafted by the insurer as adhesion, and aleatory because values exchanged can be unequal.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Interest at inception. Receipt at application. Read conditions at delivery.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which law addresses adverse action based on a consumer report?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Fair Credit Reporting Act</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-10","title":"Group, business, retirement and tax","html":"<h3>Group, business, retirement and tax</h3><p>Group life uses a master contract and member certificates. Contributory means participants share premiums. Conversion may allow a departing member to obtain individual coverage within the permitted window. Third-party ownership separates owner from insured. A life settlement sells an existing policy to a third party. Key person proceeds protect the business. Buy-sell funding finances ownership transfer. Needs analysis starts with debts, income replacement, dependents and existing assets. Social Security can pay eligible survivor and retirement benefits. Qualified plans receive tax treatment subject to federal requirements. Individual premiums are generally personal expenses, and death benefits are generally excluded subject to exceptions. Interest paid with proceeds can be taxable. Group and employer-owned coverage have separate rules. A MEC is a life contract failing the seven-pay test and generally applies income-first treatment to distributions, including loans, with possible additional tax.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Key person replaces business value. Buy-sell buys the share.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which arrangement funds a business’s purchase of a deceased owner’s interest?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Buy-sell funding</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-11","title":"Texas life rules","html":"<h3>Texas life rules</h3><p>Review Texas Insurance Code Chapters 1101, 1105, 1114, 1115, 1116, 1131 and 1153 and Pearson’s references. Identify required life provisions, grace, free look, reinstatement, incontestability, suicide and age adjustments. An illustration includes guaranteed and nonguaranteed elements. A buyer’s guide explains products and the policy summary describes the proposed contract. For a replacement, identify existing coverage, supply notices and keep records. The replacing insurer has separate notice and retention duties. For annuity recommendations, document the consumer profile, needs and reasonable basis and disclose relevant conflicts. Group law covers eligibility, certificates, conversion, assignment and dependent coverage. Credit life generally pays covered debt when the insured dies.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Replacing? Identify, disclose, compare and document.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> A new policy would lead the owner to surrender an existing contract. What should the agent evaluate?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Replacement duties and the differences in coverage and cost</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-12","title":"Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight","html":"<h3>Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</h3><p>TDI’s commissioner enforces insurance law, examines records, investigates, issues notices or orders and imposes authorized discipline. A certificate of authority concerns the insurer. Domestic, foreign and alien describe place of formation. Stock and mutual describe ownership. Authorized status concerns permission to transact insurance in Texas. Agents and agencies need appropriate licenses and appointments. Study temporary, emergency, nonresident and other license categories, exemptions, renewals, CE, records, notification and suspension or revocation under Chapters 4001–4005. For ordinary resident licensing, pass the exam, start the fingerprint process and apply through TDI’s stated process.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Authority is for the insurer. License is for the producer.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which is an insurer’s authority to transact insurance in Texas?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Certificate of authority</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-13","title":"Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection","html":"<h3>Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</h3><p>Texas Insurance Code Chapters 541, 542, 544 and 463 cover unfair trade claims, unfair settlement practices, discrimination and applicable insolvency protection. Misrepresentation falsely describes coverage. Twisting involves misleading comparisons used to induce change. Rebating concerns prohibited inducements. Defamation involves false statements about an insurer. Coercion pressures the buyer, fraud involves deception, and controlled-business rules concern licensing to write one’s own or related risks. Compare false advertising, unfair underwriting or rating, unfair claim handling, commission sharing and unlicensed referral limits. The Texas Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Association responds to qualifying member insolvencies under statutory caps and exclusions.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Describe accurately. Compare fairly. Document promptly.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> An agent lies about benefits to persuade someone to replace coverage. What is the core conduct?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Misrepresentation and potentially twisting</p></details>"}]','[{"prompt":"A client’s health declines and they want permanent coverage without a new medical exam. What feature matters?","choices":["Automatic premium loan","Reinstatement","Decreasing term","Conversion"]},{"prompt":"Which policy generally permits flexible premium funding subject to charges and lapse rules?","choices":["Universal life","Ordinary whole life","Level term","Single-premium immediate annuity"]},{"prompt":"Which contract is designed to pay after the second insured dies?","choices":["Credit life","Survivorship life","Joint first-to-die","Family income rider"]},{"prompt":"Which payout continues for a survivor under its terms?","choices":["Lump sum","Interest-only settlement","Joint-and-survivor","Single life only"]},{"prompt":"Which rider lets the owner buy more coverage at specified times without new health evidence?","choices":["Accidental death","Payor benefit","Cost-of-living rider","Guaranteed insurability"]},{"prompt":"Who generally has power to change a revocable beneficiary?","choices":["Policy owner","Contingent beneficiary","Writing agent","Medical examiner"]},{"prompt":"Which option buys a smaller permanent policy with no further premiums?","choices":["Reinstatement","Reduced paid-up","Extended term","Automatic premium loan"]},{"prompt":"Which provision generally adjusts benefits when age was recorded incorrectly?","choices":["Assignment","Payor rider","Misstatement of age","Suicide clause"]},{"prompt":"Which law addresses adverse action based on a consumer report?","choices":["COBRA","ERISA","Medicare Act","Fair Credit Reporting Act"]},{"prompt":"Which arrangement funds a business’s purchase of a deceased owner’s interest?","choices":["Buy-sell funding","Key person coverage alone","Credit life on personal debt","Dependent group life"]},{"prompt":"A new policy would lead the owner to surrender an existing contract. What should the agent evaluate?","choices":["No paperwork if the customer asked","Replacement duties and the differences in coverage and cost","Only the new premium","Only the new commission"]},{"prompt":"Which is an insurer’s authority to transact insurance in Texas?","choices":["Policy receipt","Buyer’s guide","Certificate of authority","An agent’s CE certificate"]},{"prompt":"An agent lies about benefits to persuade someone to replace coverage. What is the core conduct?","choices":["Guaranteed renewability","Nonforfeiture","Legal assignment","Misrepresentation and potentially twisting"]},{"prompt":"Which designation takes effect if no primary beneficiary can receive the proceeds?","choices":["Contingent beneficiary","Collateral assignee","Insured","Policy producer"]},{"prompt":"A client wants the same face amount for a shorter period using existing cash value. Which nonforfeiture option fits?","choices":["Automatic premium loan","Extended term","Reduced paid-up","Life income settlement"]},{"prompt":"An insured dies after retirement. Which plan can provide payments to an eligible surviving dependent under federal rules?","choices":["Health savings account eligibility","Annual renewable conversion","Social Security survivor benefits","MEC distribution test"]}]','DABCDABCDABCDABC'),
('texas-health','Texas Health Insurance License Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here","html":"<h3>Start here</h3><p>Health insurance helps pay for covered medical costs, while disability income replaces part of lost earnings and supplemental policies pay defined benefits. Start by asking what the contract pays for.</p><p>Texas tests these Health topics as part of General Lines Life, Accident, Health and HMO (InsTX-LAH05). Pair this course with the Life course for the combined exam.</p><p><a href=\"#week-one\">1-week path</a> · <a href=\"#week-two\">2-week path</a> · <a href=\"#videos\">Watch the videos</a> · <a href=\"#study-map\">Exam study map</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch the videos","html":"<h3>Watch the videos</h3><ol><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t13f66cLHVO\">TDI: How to shop for a health insurance plan</a>. Compare network, premium and cost sharing before choosing a plan.</li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=k-PWR2axm5c\">TDI: Explanation of benefits</a>. Point out the billed amount, allowed amount, plan payment and member responsibility.</li></ol>"},{"id":"week-one","title":"1-week learning path","html":"<h3>1-week learning path</h3><p>Plan about 2–3 hours daily, with more time if the material is new. Watch both videos by day 2.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1\">Medical plans and cost sharing</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-2\">Networks, accounts and managed care</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-3\">Disability, long-term care and accident</a>. Watch the first course video. Explain the terms before looking at the answer.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-4\">Group, COBRA and supplemental products</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-5\">Health policy provisions and claim clocks</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-6\">Health riders, waiting periods and renewability</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-7\">Medicare, Medicaid and Social Security</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-8\">Coordination, recovery and tax</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-9\">Field underwriting and delivery</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-10\">Texas health and ACA rules</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-11\">Texas HMO protections</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-12\">Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</a> · <a href=\"#lesson-13\">Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li>Take the graded review. Rework every miss, then use a fresh full-length timed mock. For the combined exam, complete the Life course too.</li></ol>"},{"id":"week-two","title":"2-week learning path","html":"<h3>2-week learning path</h3><p>Plan about 60–90 minutes a day. Start by recalling yesterday’s rule and end with the lesson check. Allow extra time for a fresh timed mock.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1\">Medical plans and cost sharing</a>. Watch the first course video. Explain the terms before looking at the answer.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2\">Networks, accounts and managed care</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3\">Disability, long-term care and accident</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-4\">Group, COBRA and supplemental products</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-5\">Health policy provisions and claim clocks</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-6\">Health riders, waiting periods and renewability</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-7\">Medicare, Medicaid and Social Security</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-8\">Coordination, recovery and tax</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-9\">Field underwriting and delivery</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-10\">Texas health and ACA rules</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-11\">Texas HMO protections</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-12\">Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li><a href=\"#lesson-13\">Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</a>. Answer the recall question closed-book and explain the clue that changes the outcome.</li><li>Take the graded review. Rework every miss, then use a fresh full-length timed mock. For the combined exam, complete the Life course too.</li></ol>"},{"id":"study-map","title":"Exam study map and references","html":"<h3>Exam study map and references</h3><p>Pearson groups health material into policies, provisions, social insurance, other concepts, and field underwriting. Use lessons 1–4, 5–6, 7, 8 and 9 respectively. Study lessons 10–13 for Texas law and HMOs.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.pearsonvue.com/content/dam/VUE/vue/en/documents/publications/124401.pdf\">Pearson VUE official Texas content outlines</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/agent/general-life-apply.html\">TDI application steps</a></li><li><a href=\"https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/\">Texas statutes</a></li><li><a href=\"https://tsinational.com/product/life-health-annuity-agent-licensing-tx/\">TSI National study resources</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.xcelsolutions.com/texas/insurance-license/life-and-health\">XCEL study resources</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.examfx.com/hubfs/L%26H_10-Day_Pearson_Cert.pdf\">ExamFX study calendar and simulation approach</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"lesson-1","title":"Medical plans and cost sharing","html":"<h3>Medical plans and cost sharing</h3><p>Basic hospital, surgical and medical policies often pay defined benefits. Major medical covers a broader range of eligible costs. The premium buys coverage. A deductible is the amount paid before specified benefits begin, a copay is a flat amount, and coinsurance is a percentage. Use the allowed charge. A $1,000 eligible claim with $200 deductible remaining and 20% coinsurance costs $360 before any applicable out-of-pocket cap. Premiums and excluded services typically do not count toward that cap.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Deduct first. Split the rest.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> What is 20% coinsurance on a $600 allowed claim after the deductible is met?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>$120</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-2","title":"Networks, accounts and managed care","html":"<h3>Networks, accounts and managed care</h3><p>HMO designs use a provider network and may require a primary care provider or referral, with exceptions such as covered emergency services. PPOs typically allow out-of-network care at higher cost. POS plans combine coordinated care and an out-of-network option. Preauthorization is advance review and does not by itself guarantee payment. URC means usual, reasonable and customary and can affect reimbursement. HSA contributions require eligibility including a qualifying HDHP. FSAs are employer-plan accounts with plan-specific carryover or grace rules. HRAs are employer funded.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> HSA has eligibility. HRA has employer funding.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which account is employer funded by design?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>HRA</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-3","title":"Disability, long-term care and accident","html":"<h3>Disability, long-term care and accident</h3><p>Individual disability insurance can replace income. Group disability uses a master policy. Own-occupation and any-occupation definitions ask different questions. Partial disability concerns reduced capacity. Residual disability usually uses income loss. Recurrent disability can link to an earlier claim under the policy. Overhead expense pays eligible business expenses, disability buyout funds ownership transfer, and key employee coverage protects the business. AD&amp;D covers specified accidental loss. LTC may pay for home, assisted living or nursing services. Tax-qualified LTC commonly uses inability to perform at least two activities of daily living or severe cognitive impairment under applicable certification rules.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Income pays the worker. Overhead keeps the office open. Buyout buys the share.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which policy addresses rent for an office while its owner is disabled?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Business overhead expense</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-4","title":"Group, COBRA and supplemental products","html":"<h3>Group, COBRA and supplemental products</h3><p>A group master policy belongs to the group policyholder and eligible members receive evidence of coverage. COBRA provides temporary continuation for qualifying events and eligible plans. Check eligibility, election and payment timelines. Dental and vision plans focus on stated services. Cancer and critical illness pay for defined diagnoses or expenses. Hospital indemnity commonly pays a fixed benefit. Worksite coverage is distributed through employers, and accident policies cover defined accidental injuries. Short-term medical has narrower protections than ACA-compliant coverage and product rules vary. Compare benefits, exclusions and limits before presenting alternatives.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Indemnity pays a set benefit. Major medical pays eligible expenses under the plan.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which policy typically pays a specified daily cash benefit for a qualifying hospital stay?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Hospital indemnity</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-5","title":"Health policy provisions and claim clocks","html":"<h3>Health policy provisions and claim clocks</h3><p>The entire contract defines controlling documents and the insuring clause states the benefit promise. Consideration includes premium and required application statements. Grace covers a late premium interval and reinstatement restores eligible lapsed coverage. Time limits on certain defenses control some coverage challenges. The claim sequence is notice, forms, proof, determination and payment. The policy governs who is paid. Physical exams and autopsy provisions address insurer verification. Legal action clauses specify when suit can be filed. Beneficiary change and age or sex misstatements follow contract and law. Optional occupation, illegal occupation and relation-of-earnings provisions can affect benefits. Verify applicable deadlines in Pearson’s cited provisions and the approved form.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Notice tells. Proof supports. Payment follows.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which document substantiates the amount or facts of a claim after notice?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Proof of loss</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-6","title":"Health riders, waiting periods and renewability","html":"<h3>Health riders, waiting periods and renewability</h3><p>A probationary period starts at policy inception and can exclude early sickness. An elimination period is the waiting time after disability begins before benefits accrue under the policy. Waiver of premium relieves qualifying premium obligations. Impairment riders exclude stated conditions where allowed. Guaranteed insurability and future increase options permit defined additions. Noncancelable generally locks renewability and premium under terms. Guaranteed renewable protects renewal but allows class-based rate increases. Cancelable permits termination under contract and legal rules. Apply preexisting-condition and dollar-limit restrictions based on the product. ACA protections apply to qualifying major medical coverage.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Probation starts at issue. Elimination starts at disability.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which provision protects renewability while allowing class-wide premium increases?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Guaranteed renewable</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-7","title":"Medicare, Medicaid and Social Security","html":"<h3>Medicare, Medicaid and Social Security</h3><p>Medicare A generally covers inpatient hospital and certain related services, B medical and outpatient services, C Medicare Advantage, and D prescription drugs. A Medigap policy supplements Original Medicare cost sharing under its standardized benefits and is distinct from Advantage. Medicaid is federal-state coverage based on eligibility. Social Security disability uses its own eligibility and work requirements and is distinct from private disability insurance. Verify Medicare enrollment and supplement rules before recommending replacement.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> A admission, B bills, C choice, D drugs.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which part normally covers outpatient prescription drug benefits through a drug plan?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Part D</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-8","title":"Coordination, recovery and tax","html":"<h3>Coordination, recovery and tax</h3><p>The primary plan pays first and secondary applies its coordination terms. Nonduplication limits duplicate recoveries. Subrogation allows a plan with valid rights to seek reimbursement from a liable third party. Occupational coverage concerns work-related incidents. Workers’ compensation can provide medical and income benefits for qualifying work injuries under state law. Premium mode is frequency, and the policyowner holds contractual rights. Primary beneficiaries precede contingent beneficiaries, and dependent coverage depends on eligibility. Disability benefits are generally taxable to the extent funded with employer or pre-tax premiums and generally tax-free when individually paid after tax. Medical expense tax treatment is separate.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Primary pays first. Subrogation seeks repayment.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which plan processes an eligible expense first under coordination rules?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Primary plan</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-9","title":"Field underwriting and delivery","html":"<h3>Field underwriting and delivery</h3><p>Complete the application and signatures, explain permitted use of health information and consumer reports and submit any collected premium promptly. A conditional receipt must be read before promising an effective date. MIB contributes underwriting information. FCRA controls permissible reports and applicable adverse-action notices. Follow privacy authorization and consent requirements. At delivery explain ratings, exclusions, riders and the effective date. Compare existing and proposed terms before replacement. Contract elements are agreement, consideration, competent parties and legal purpose. Insurance is conditional, unilateral, adhesion and aleatory. A representation states information and a warranty is a contractual assurance. Insurable interest connects the coverage to actual exposure.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Application, underwriting, issue, delivery.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> A customer pays an initial premium with a conditional receipt. What determines whether coverage starts?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The receipt’s conditions and the insurer’s rules</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-10","title":"Texas health and ACA rules","html":"<h3>Texas health and ACA rules</h3><p>Use Texas Insurance Code Chapters 1367, 1368, 1501 and 1551 and the 28 TAC sections in Pearson’s outline. Review newborn coverage, dependent definitions, chemical-dependency benefits, small-group eligibility, LTC product standards, HIV testing consent and Medicare supplement minimums and cancellation. Marketplace plans use HealthCare.gov in Texas. Premium tax credits and cost-sharing reductions have distinct eligibility rules. Essential health benefits include mental health and substance use, pediatric services and preventive services. Review employer Marketplace notice duties and apply ACA protections within their scope.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Premium tax credit lowers premium. Cost-sharing reduction lowers eligible costs when using a qualifying plan.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which benefit category includes covered treatment for substance use disorders?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Mental health and substance use disorder services</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-11","title":"Texas HMO protections","html":"<h3>Texas HMO protections</h3><p>Texas Insurance Code Chapters 843 and 1271 and 28 TAC Chapter 11 address HMO organization, enrollment, cancellation, nonrenewal, evidence of coverage and out-of-network claims. Evidence of coverage explains covered services, exclusions, member obligations and how to obtain care. Before a claim, identify emergency versus routine care and any network exception. Review state and federal surprise-billing protections and the cited rule before deciding what the enrollee owes.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Evidence describes. Network organizes. Emergency rules protect.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which document describes the enrolled member’s services and how to obtain them?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Evidence of coverage</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-12","title":"Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight","html":"<h3>Texas licensing, conduct and insurer oversight</h3><p>TDI’s commissioner enforces insurance law, examines records, investigates, issues notices or orders and imposes authorized discipline. A certificate of authority concerns the insurer. Domestic, foreign and alien describe place of formation. Stock and mutual describe ownership. Authorized status concerns permission to transact insurance in Texas. Agents and agencies need appropriate licenses and appointments. Study temporary, emergency, nonresident and other license categories, exemptions, renewals, CE, records, notification and suspension or revocation under Chapters 4001–4005. For ordinary resident licensing, pass the exam, start the fingerprint process and apply through TDI’s stated process.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Authority is for the insurer. License is for the producer.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> Which is an insurer’s authority to transact insurance in Texas?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Certificate of authority</p></details>"},{"id":"lesson-13","title":"Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection","html":"<h3>Texas unfair practices and guaranty protection</h3><p>Texas Insurance Code Chapters 541, 542, 544 and 463 cover unfair trade claims, unfair settlement practices, discrimination and applicable insolvency protection. Misrepresentation falsely describes coverage. Twisting involves misleading comparisons used to induce change. Rebating concerns prohibited inducements. Defamation involves false statements about an insurer. Coercion pressures the buyer, fraud involves deception, and controlled-business rules concern licensing to write one’s own or related risks. Compare false advertising, unfair underwriting or rating, unfair claim handling, commission sharing and unlicensed referral limits. The Texas Life and Health Insurance Guaranty Association responds to qualifying member insolvencies under statutory caps and exclusions.</p><blockquote><strong>Memory cue</strong> Describe accurately. Compare fairly. Document promptly.</blockquote><p><strong>Recall</strong> An agent lies about benefits to persuade someone to replace coverage. What is the core conduct?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Misrepresentation and potentially twisting</p></details>"}]','[{"prompt":"What is 20% coinsurance on a $600 allowed claim after the deductible is met?","choices":["$480","$600","$0","$120"]},{"prompt":"Which account is employer funded by design?","choices":["HRA","HSA exclusively","Roth IRA","Individual Medicare account"]},{"prompt":"Which policy addresses rent for an office while its owner is disabled?","choices":["Medicare supplement","Business overhead expense","Individual hospital indemnity","Disability buyout"]},{"prompt":"Which policy typically pays a specified daily cash benefit for a qualifying hospital stay?","choices":["Medigap by definition","Workers’ compensation","Hospital indemnity","PPO major medical"]},{"prompt":"Which document substantiates the amount or facts of a claim after notice?","choices":["Premium schedule","Provider directory","Outline of coverage","Proof of loss"]},{"prompt":"Which provision protects renewability while allowing class-wide premium increases?","choices":["Guaranteed renewable","Noncancelable","Cancelable","Free look"]},{"prompt":"Which part normally covers outpatient prescription drug benefits through a drug plan?","choices":["Medicaid automatically","Part D","Part A","Part B exclusively"]},{"prompt":"Which plan processes an eligible expense first under coordination rules?","choices":["Plan with smaller premium","Plan with more dependents","Primary plan","Secondary plan"]},{"prompt":"A customer pays an initial premium with a conditional receipt. What determines whether coverage starts?","choices":["Payment alone always","The agent’s verbal estimate","The date of first illness alone","The receipt’s conditions and the insurer’s rules"]},{"prompt":"Which benefit category includes covered treatment for substance use disorders?","choices":["Mental health and substance use disorder services","Credit disability","Hospital indemnity only","Nonforfeiture"]},{"prompt":"Which document describes the enrolled member’s services and how to obtain them?","choices":["Guaranty assessment","Evidence of coverage","Producer appointment","Provider advertising flyer"]},{"prompt":"Which is an insurer’s authority to transact insurance in Texas?","choices":["Policy receipt","Buyer’s guide","Certificate of authority","An agent’s CE certificate"]},{"prompt":"An agent lies about benefits to persuade someone to replace coverage. What is the core conduct?","choices":["Guaranteed renewability","Nonforfeiture","Legal assignment","Misrepresentation and potentially twisting"]},{"prompt":"A plan has charged a $75 flat fee for an eligible visit. What kind of cost sharing is that?","choices":["Copayment","Coinsurance","Annual premium","Employer HRA contribution"]},{"prompt":"A disability starts today and benefits begin after 90 days. Which clock is running?","choices":["Grace period","Elimination period","Free look period","Probationary period at issue"]},{"prompt":"What policy is designed to help pay specified cost sharing under Original Medicare?","choices":["Disability buyout","Hospital indemnity by definition","Medicare supplement","Part C automatically"]}]','DABCDABCDABCDABC');
